from helpers import *

def part3(D):
    h = ['<h1 class="part" id="p3"><small>Part 3</small>Primitive &amp; Improvised Tools</h1>']
    h.append('<p>Steel tools will be around for a long time after a collapse, but knowing how to make tools from stone, bone, wood and fiber fills gaps — a lost knife on a hunting trip, a need for many scrapers, or cordage for a hundred uses. These skills also make you better at using and repairing modern tools. The U.S. Army survival manual (ATP 3-50.21) covers field-expedient tools in detail.</p>')
    h.append('<h2 id="stone">3.1 Stone tools and knapping basics</h2>')
    h.append('<p><b>Knappable stone</b> breaks with a smooth, shell-like (conchoidal) fracture, like thick glass: <b>flint and chert</b> (found as nodules and layers in limestone and chalk, and as river cobbles), <b>obsidian</b> (volcanic glass — the sharpest edge known, but brittle), <b>chalcedony, jasper, agate, novaculite</b>, and fine-grained <b>quartzite and basalt</b>. Test a candidate stone by striking a thin edge: it should ring, not thud, and the break should be glassy and smooth, not grainy. Where no stone is available, thick bottle glass and porcelain insulators knap well.</p>')
    h.append(table(["Technique", "Tool", "Use"], [
        ["Hard-hammer percussion", "Rounded hammerstone (quartzite cobble)", "Strike large flakes from a core; rough out blanks (bifaces)"],
        ["Soft-hammer percussion", "Antler billet, dense hardwood baton, copper billet", "Thinning bifaces; flatter, longer flakes"],
        ["Pressure flaking", "Antler tine or copper-tipped stick", "Pushing off small flakes to finish edges, notches and points"],
        ["Abrading", "Coarse sandstone", "Grinding the platform edge so it does not crush; dulling edges where they will be hafted"]]))
    h.append('<p><b>Method:</b> sit with a thick leather pad on your thigh and a leather pad in your palm. Strike near the edge of the stone at a steep angle (less than 90° between the striking surface and the face you want to remove); the flake comes off the face below the blow. Simple sharp <b>flakes</b> are excellent knives and scrapers without any further work. A <b>hand axe or celt</b> (ground stone axe) can be made by pecking a tough stone (basalt, diorite, greenstone) into shape with a hammerstone and grinding it on wet sandstone — slow, but such axes fell trees and do not shatter.</p>')
    h.append(danger("Knapping hazards", "Flakes are razor-sharp and fly: wear safety glasses, leather gloves/pads and long pants. Knap outdoors over a tarp — the fine dust from flint and quartzite is crystalline silica, which scars the lungs if breathed regularly; never knap indoors without ventilation and sweep wet, not dry. Many quarry sites (Alibates, Flint Ridge, Obsidian Cliff, Knife River) are protected archaeological sites: do not dig or collect there."))
    h.append('<h2 id="bone">3.2 Bone, antler and shell</h2>')
    h.append('<p>Fresh, dense leg bones of deer, elk, cattle or moose are tough and easily shaped. Split them by scoring with a stone flake and snapping, or by sawing; grind to shape on sandstone. Bone makes <b>awls and needles</b> (for leather and nets), <b>fish hooks and gorges</b>, scrapers for hides, <b>chisels and wedges</b>, and punches. <b>Antler</b> is tougher than bone and makes the best knapping billets, pressure flakers, wedges for splitting logs, and tool handles. Soak antler in water for a day to soften it before carving. Shell (clams, conch in the Caribbean) gives scrapers, spoons and blades.</p>')
    h.append('<h2 id="woodtools">3.3 Wood tools</h2>')
    h.append('<ul><li><b>Digging stick:</b> straight hardwood 3–4 ft long, point fire-hardened (char lightly over coals and scrape — repeat; do not burn deeply). The basic gardening tool of many cultures.</li><li><b>Mallets and mauls:</b> a section of dense wood (hop-hornbeam, oak, hickory, dogwood, mesquite, burls) with the handle carved from the same piece. Use to drive chisels, froes and wedges.</li><li><b>Wedges (gluts):</b> split from dense dry hardwood; drive with a wooden maul to split logs into rails, boards and shingles.</li><li><b>Rakes, pitchforks, shovels:</b> forks from a naturally branched sapling (steam- or heat-bend and tie while drying); shovels from split boards with edges protected by sheet-metal straps.</li><li><b>Spoons, bowls, trays:</b> carved green from birch, maple, basswood, alder, cherry; hollow by burning with coals and scraping.</li></ul>')
    h.append('<h2 id="cordage">3.4 Cordage</h2>')
    h.append('<p>Strong cord is made by <b>reverse-wrap twisting</b>: twist a bundle of fibers until it kinks into two strands, then twist each strand in one direction while wrapping them around each other in the opposite direction. Splice in new fiber before a strand runs out. Two-ply cord is much stronger than the same fiber unspun.</p>')
    h.append(table(["Fiber source", "Where", "Notes"], [
        ["Dogbane (Indian hemp), milkweed stalks", "Most of the U.S. and southern Canada", "Collect dead stalks in fall; crush, peel outer fiber. Some of the strongest plant cord"],
        ["Stinging nettle", "Damp woods across North America", "Strip fiber from dried stalks (wear gloves)"],
        ["Inner bark: basswood, willow, cedar, elm, mulberry", "Forests; cedar in PNW", "Soak (ret) basswood bark in water 2–4 weeks to separate fibers"],
        ["Yucca, agave (ixtle, henequén, sisal)", "Southwest, Mexico, Yucatán", "Pound or scrape leaves; agave fiber is the source of sisal rope"],
        ["Rawhide, sinew", "Any large animal", "Sinew (back strap, leg tendons) is excellent lashing; shrinks tight when dry"],
        ["Salvaged", "Anywhere", "Paracord inner strands, baling twine, wire, plastic bags cut into strips, seat-belt webbing"]]))
    h.append('<h2 id="horse">3.5 Shaving horse and spring-pole lathe</h2>')
    h.append('<p>A <b>shaving horse</b> is a bench with a foot-operated clamp that holds wood while you shape it with a drawknife or spokeshave: tool handles, pegs, shingles, chair legs, rake teeth, bows. A <b>spring-pole lathe</b> turns wood for tool handles, bowls and spindles using a springy sapling and a foot treadle — no electricity. Both can be built from split logs, a few augered holes and pegs.</p>')
    h.append(fig(D.shaving_horse(), "<b>Figure 4. Shaving horse (A) and spring-pole lathe (B).</b> Build the horse bench from a split half-log ~6 ft long on three or four splayed legs. The lathe’s centers are pointed steel pins (large nails filed to a point) in two uprights; the tool rests on a bar in front of the work."))
    return "\n".join(h)

def part4a(D):
    h = ['<h1 class="part" id="p4"><small>Part 4</small>Blacksmithing</h1>']
    h.append('<p>Blacksmithing turns scrap steel into the things a community constantly needs: hooks, hinges, nails, chains, tools, knives, and repairs for farm equipment. A basic shop needs only a <b>forge</b> (fire + air), an <b>anvil</b> (any heavy hard steel), <b>hammers</b>, <b>tongs</b>, a <b>vise</b>, and water. The Artist-Blacksmith’s Association of North America (ABANA) and its local affiliates across the U.S. and Canada run classes — learning from a smith now is worth more than any book.</p>')
    h.append('<h2 id="forges">4.1 Forge types and fuels</h2>')
    h.append(table(["Forge", "Fuel", "Pros", "Cons"], [
        ["Solid-fuel side- or bottom-blast", "Bituminous <b>blacksmith coal</b> (low sulfur) → burns to coke", "Very hot, small intense fire; fuel stores forever; can forge-weld easily", "Smoky until coked; clinker; coal must be found or stockpiled"],
        ["Solid-fuel (charcoal)", "Hardwood or softwood <b>charcoal</b>", "Fuel can be made anywhere there are trees; clean, no sulfur — excellent for welding", "Uses 2–3× the volume of coal; sparks; needs a deeper, larger firepot"],
        ["Propane (gas)", "Propane, with a venturi or blower burner", "Clean, quick to light, even heat, easy to learn", "Fuel runs out and cannot be made at home; burner parts; hard to weld long pieces"],
        ["Improvised", "Wood fire with forced air, coal in a pit", "Zero cost; a hole in the ground with a pipe works", "Inefficient; wood alone (without charcoaling) is a poor forge fuel"]]))
    h.append(tip("Fuel facts", "Use metallurgical blacksmith coal if you can get it — high-sulfur coal makes steel brittle and hot-short. A 50 lb (23 kg) bag of coal lasts a beginner several days of forging. Charcoal: expect to burn roughly a 5-gallon bucket per hour of steady forging; make it by the barrel-load (section 4.3). Hard lump charcoal from oak, hickory, maple or mesquite lasts longest; softwood charcoal burns faster but is fine with gentler air."))
    h.append('<h2 id="drum">4.2 Build a brake-drum forge</h2>')
    h.append('<p>The brake-drum forge is the classic beginner build: a cast-iron vehicle brake drum is a ready-made firepot that tolerates heat. Truck drums (15–16 in diameter) hold a bigger fire than car drums.</p>')
    h.append(fig(D.brake_drum_forge(), "<b>Figure 5. Brake-drum forge, cross section.</b> The air pipe (tuyere) enters from below; a removable cap on the tee lets ash and clinker drop out.", "max-width:92%"))
    h.append(check("Brake-drum forge parts and steps", [
        "Brake drum; wash off grease and brake dust with water (wet-wipe — old brakes may contain asbestos; never blow the dust off)",
        "Steel table or frame at a height where the fire is about at your wrist with arms hanging; a steel wheel rim or old grill also works",
        "1½–2 in BLACK steel pipe: floor flange, short nipple, tee, 6–8 in nipple down to a threaded cap (ash dump), side pipe 2–3 ft to the blower",
        "Grate: ¼ in steel plate with 5–7 holes of ½ in drilled over the drum hole, or a few bolts/rebar pieces across the hole",
        "Air: hand-crank blower, a large bellows, an old hair dryer or vacuum on 'blow', or inflatable-toy blower; a gate valve or sliding plate to throttle it",
        "Optional: line the drum with a mix of clay and sand (about 50/50) to shape a firepot 4–5 in deep; let it dry slowly",
        "Light with paper and kindling over the grate, add fuel around it, start low air. Bank wet coal around the edge (coking ring) and push it in as coke"]))
    h.append(danger("Never use galvanized pipe or fittings in a forge", "Galvanized pipe (dull gray, crystalline “spangled” zinc coating) gives off zinc oxide fumes when hot, causing metal fume fever. Use black iron pipe or remove the coating first (soak in vinegar outdoors until fizzing stops). See Part 7."))
    h.append('<h2 id="charcoal">4.3 Making charcoal</h2>')
    h.append('<p>Charcoal is wood heated without enough air to burn: the water and tars drive off as smoke and gas, leaving nearly pure carbon. The UN Food and Agriculture Organization (FAO) manuals on simple charcoal making describe pits, earth mounds, metal kilns and retorts. For a household smithy, a <b>double-barrel retort</b> gives the best yield and the cleanest charcoal; a <b>pit</b> needs no equipment.</p>')
    h.append(fig(D.charcoal_retort(), "<b>Figure 6. Charcoal retort (A) and pit (B).</b> In the retort, gases from the inner drum are piped back into the firebox and burn, heating the wood with its own gas."))
    h.append('<ul><li><b>Wood:</b> dry (seasoned 6+ months), split to 1–3 in (2.5–7.5 cm) thick, cut to fit. Dense hardwoods make denser, longer-burning charcoal. Do not use painted, treated or plywood scraps — toxic fumes and contaminants.</li><li><b>Signs of progress:</b> white steam (drying) → thick yellow-brown smoke (tar, the wood is cooking) → thin bluish smoke and a jet of flame at the gas pipe → smoke stops: finished. A 30-gallon inner drum takes about 3–6 hours.</li><li><b>Cooling:</b> seal every opening with earth or clay and let it cool completely, overnight or longer. Opening hot charcoal to air can make it burst into flame.</li><li><b>Good charcoal</b> is black, light, rings when tapped, and breaks with a shiny surface. Brown, soft ends (“brands”) are undercooked — put them back in next batch. Sort into egg-to-walnut size for the forge.</li></ul>')
    h.append(danger("Charcoal making makes carbon monoxide and starts wildfires", "Run retorts and pits outdoors, downwind of homes, on bare mineral soil with a 10 ft (3 m) cleared circle, and never on windy, dry days. Keep water and a shovel at hand. Store finished charcoal in metal containers — fresh charcoal can self-heat and ignite if piled while still warm or damp. Never burn charcoal indoors or in a closed tent or garage to cook or heat."))
    h.append('<h2 id="layout">4.4 Forge shop layout</h2>')
    h.append(fig(D.forge_layout(), "<b>Figure 7. A safe working layout (plan view).</b> Forge, anvil and quench should be within one step and a turn of each other, under a roof with open sides for ventilation."))
    h.append('<h2 id="anvil">4.5 Anvils, hammers and tongs</h2>')
    h.append('<p>An anvil only has to be <b>heavy, hard and solidly mounted</b>. The rule of thumb is that the anvil should weigh about 50 times the hammer: 100+ lb (45 kg) is ideal for general work, but much less works for small items. Set the face at about knuckle height with your arms hanging, on a hardwood stump or a steel stand filled with sand. Test with a ball bearing dropped from 10 in (25 cm): it should rebound most of the way; a low, dead bounce means a soft or cracked face.</p>')
    h.append(table(["Improvised anvil", "How to use it", "Notes"], [
        ["<b>Railroad rail, stood on end</b>", "Cut 12–24 in (30–60 cm) section; stand vertically in a stump or bucket of concrete; strike on the end of the head", "All the mass is under the hammer: better than lying flat. Grind the end flat and smooth"],
        ["Railroad rail, lying flat", "Grind a horn into one end; drill a hardy hole if possible", "Rings loudly — chain it down or set it on silicone/sand; rebound is poor in the middle"],
        ["Steel block / drop", "Any big hunk: forklift tine, large shaft or axle end, bulldozer parts, a 4×4 in square bar", "Forklift tines are tough medium-carbon steel and make excellent anvils"],
        ["Sledgehammer head", "Bury the head face-up in a stump", "Good for small work, nails, chain"],
        ["Large boulder", "Flat, hard, fine-grained stone (granite, basalt)", "Historic last resort; may crack"]]))
    h.append('<p><b>Hammers:</b> start with a 2–2½ lb (0.9–1.1 kg) cross-peen; the peen moves metal in one direction (drawing). A ball-peen and a 4 lb hammer cover most other work. Dress the face of salvaged hammers so the edges are rounded — sharp edges leave marks and can chip. <b>Tongs</b> must hold the work securely: a bad grip throws hot steel. Start with a pair of flat-jaw tongs and a pair of V-bit (for round and square bar); make others as the first projects (section 4.11). Vise-grips work for short jobs but lose their temper if overheated. A <b>post (leg) vise</b> is the best vise for hammering; a machinist’s bench vise can crack if struck hard.</p>')
    return "\n".join(h)
